 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
-- Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the remaining tabs to t1..tn
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: `closeTab` renumbers the remaining tabs `t1…tn` in their current order and resets `next` to `n + 1`. Keys are
+  always contiguous (`t1,t2`) and `openTab` after a close takes the next contiguous key, never a gap or a duplicate.
+  Closing a key that is not open returns the state unchanged.
+- Forbidden: leaving a gap after a close (`t1,t3`), or letting `next` differ from `tabs.length + 1`.
+- Reason: overridden by the maintainer on 2026-10-23, superseding §stable-tab-keys; the maintainer wants the strip and
+  the `?tabs=` URL state to stay contiguous.
+- Trade-off accepted: the key is still the React key and the URL state, so closing a tab remounts every tab to the right
+  of it (they get new keys) and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input — the incident of 2026-08-29 is a known
+  cost again. Old `?tabs=` URLs and any key held across a close (bookmarks, handlers, maps keyed by tab key) now point at
+  a different tab or at none. A separate stable id for the React key would avoid the remount; that is not done here.
 - Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+
+### §stable-tab-keys — SUPERSEDED 2026-10-23 by §contiguous-tab-keys (closing a tab never renumbers the others)
+- Since: 2026-09-02
+- Superseded: 2026-10-23, overridden by the maintainer. No longer in force; kept so the reason is not lost.
+- Was decided: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
+- Was forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
+- Why it existed: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed
+  one and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the old "close keeps keys"
+  test, since replaced by the renumbering tests).
+- Evidence: none; the "close keeps keys" test was replaced on 2026-10-23.
